@h1 Literal & raw nodes; custom CSS h1@

A raw node embeds content @strong verbatim strong@ — code, foreign markup, a
themed stylesheet. Doubling the marker to @@@code @@name code@@@ freezes Wit
syntax inside the body; tripling it to @@@code @@@name code@@@ freezes even the
@@@code {{…}} code@@@ interpolation holes. The freeze level is how you choose how
much processing the body receives.

@h2 A literal body h2@

Inside @@@code @@name … name@@ code@@@, every Wit construct is inert — an
@@@code @ code@@@, @@@code # code@@@, @code _ code@, @code * code@, or
@@@code ::hole:: code@@@ is just text:

@@@pre
@@code
let x = @not_a_node;
code@@
pre@@@

That renders the line literally, @@@code @not_a_node code@@@ and all.

@h2 Block or inline h2@

A raw node is a @strong block strong@ when a newline directly follows the opening
@@@code @@name code@@@, and @strong inline strong@ otherwise. The first literal
@@@code name@@ code@@@ closes it; if none appears, that is
@code E_UNCLOSED_RAW_NODE code@. One whitespace character at each boundary is
trimmed.

@h2 Names map to elements h2@

A raw node named after a known HTML tag emits that element — @@@code @@@code
code@@@ becomes @code <code> code@, @@@code @@pre code@@@ becomes
@code <pre> code@. A custom name becomes a @code <div> code@ carrying
@code class="wit-node" code@ and a @code data-wit-name code@ attribute. Bodies
are HTML-escaped, @strong except strong@ for @@@code @@style code@@@ and
@@@code @@script code@@@, which are emitted as raw text (with a break-out guard
against a stray closing tag).

@h2 The three freeze levels h2@

@ul
@li @strong One marker strong@ — @@@code @name code@@@ — is an ordinary node:
live Wit, and no interpolation (prose is not an interpolation context). li@
@li @strong Two markers strong@ — @@@code @@name code@@@ — freezes Wit but keeps
@@@code {{…}} code@@@ @strong live strong@. li@
@li @strong Three markers strong@ — @@@code @@@name code@@@ — freezes both. li@
ul@

This is why @@@code {{path}} code@@@ is @strong live only inside a raw node
body strong@ — it is the one place interpolation resolves.

@h2 Themed CSS with interpolation h2@

The flagship use: drive a stylesheet from a data definition.

@@@pre
#theme: { accent - green }

@@style
.badge { color: {{theme.accent}}; }
style@@
pre@@@

That renders @@@code .badge { color: green; } code@@@ — the
@@@code {{theme.accent}} code@@@ resolves because @@@code @@style code@@@ is a
two-marker raw node. Triple it and the hole stays literal:

@@@pre
@@@code {{x}} code@@@   ~ renders the literal text {{x}}
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 Freezing has a cost: a frozen @@@code @@table code@@@ will @strong not strong@ resolve @@@code @ref code@@@ rows from data. When a table reads from your data, use a plain @@@code @table code@@@ instead. aside@

@h2 Not shipped: component defs h2@

You may see @@@code ##name … name## code@@@ raw-component definitions in older
notes. They are @strong roadmap, not shipped strong@ — there is no
@@@code ## code@@@ token today. Do not rely on them.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Expecting @@@code {{…}} code@@@ to be live in prose — it is live only inside
a raw node body. li@
@li Freezing a table you want resolved from data. li@
@li Documenting @@@code ## code@@@ component defs as if they shipped. li@
ul@

